.TH TTYNAME 3
.SH NAME
ttyname, isatty, ttyslot \- find name of a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <unistd.h>
.PP
.B char *ttyname(int fildes);
.PP
.B int isatty(int fildes);
.PP
.B int ttyslot(void);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ttyname
returns a pointer to the null-terminated path name
of the terminal device associated with file descriptor
.IR fildes .
.PP
.I Isatty
returns 1 if
.I fildes
is associated with a terminal device, 0 otherwise.
.PP
.I Ttyslot
returns the number of the entry in the
.IR ttys (5)
file for the control terminal of the
current process.
.PP
.B Note:
none of the three is declared by a header \- v7 had none and C11 has no such
routines \- so a caller declares them itself.
.SH FILES
/dev/*
.br
/etc/ttys
.br
share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/gen/ttyname.c, isatty.c, ttyslot.c
.SH SEE ALSO
ioctl(2), ttys(5), getlogin(3), stty(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Ttyname
returns a null pointer (0) if
.I fildes
does not describe a terminal device in directory `/dev'.
.PP
.I Ttyslot
returns 0 if `/etc/ttys' is inaccessible or if
it cannot determine the control terminal.
.SH BUGS
The return value points to static data
whose content is overwritten by each call.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Reading a directory is an ordinary read(2) here
As it was in v7, and as this kernel allows.  The consequence is worth knowing
before writing a test:
.B ttyname works under the kernel and not under b6sim,
whose
.I read
is the host's and refuses a directory.
.I Ttyslot
and
.IR getlogin (3)
stand on it and are the same story.  Their failure paths are what the b6sim
suite covers; the rest is exercised off the disk image.
.PP
The directory buffer is sized from
.B DIRSIZ
rather than v7's flat 32, since DIRSIZ is
.B 18
here and not 14.
.SS isatty
.B struct sgttyb is five words here
\- four chars, each occupying a word of its own, and an
.BR int .
That is the shape
.IR gtty (2)
fills, and there is no packing to undo.
.SS Two v7 bugs fixed rather than carried
.I Ttyname
copied the directory entry onto the end of "/dev/" with an unbounded
.IR strcat ,
which a long enough entry overran.
.PP
.I Ttyslot
tested for the end of its buffer
.I after
storing the character, so a line of exactly 32 characters wrote one past the
array.
